60,3,1,talk,Misaki,,"Considering the risks of financial friction and the maintenance burden of racks, the Annual Lottery seems the most stable. It breaks the long-term monopoly on spots without damaging our community bonds. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Shizue,,I agree with Misaki. A lottery is a gentle way to ensure everyone has a fair chance at the dry spots without adding stress to our neighbors or our treasurer. I vote for Option C.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Misaki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,May,,"I'll go with the flow! While I was curious about the money, the risk of people skipping meetings is a total mood-killer. Let's keep it simple and fair. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,George,,"The evidence is clear. Option A is an administrative burden, and Option B is a maintenance liability. Option C is the only sustainable choice that ensures fairness without creating new problems. I vote for Option C.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Mio,,"I'm totally on board! It's the best way to stop the ""same old places"" problem and keep everyone happy. Let's make it fair for everyone! I vote for Option C.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
